<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/fargo-depot.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/fargo-depot-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/fargo-depot.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="799" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/across-north-dakota/">Across North Dakota</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Fargo</span></nav><p class="kicker">Red River Valley</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser in Fargo, North Dakota</h1><p class="lede">Fargo homeowners and building owners deal with hail, wind, deep cold and the Red River every year. When a carrier accepts a claim but the estimate falls short, we review the numbers and represent you through the appraisal clause in your policy.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line" href="/request-an-appraisal/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Cass County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Red River Valley</dd></div><div><dt>Housing</dt><dd>Prewar bungalows, postwar ranches, NDSU rentals &amp; south-side subdivisions</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="localbox"><p class="localbox-t">Local claim risks</p><ul class="chips"><li>Large hail</li><li>Straight-line wind</li><li>Frozen pipes</li><li>Ice dams</li></ul><p class="localbox-n"><b>Areas we see:</b> Downtown, Hawthorne, Horace Mann, Clara Barton, Island Park, Roosevelt near NDSU, Osgood &amp; newer south Fargo</p></div>
<h2>Insurance appraisal for Fargo property claims</h2>
<p>Fargo is the largest city in North Dakota and the seat of Cass County, and its housing tells the story of a century of growth on flat Red River Valley clay. Close to downtown, the Hawthorne, Horace Mann and Clara Barton neighborhoods hold two-story foursquares, bungalows and brick homes from the early 1900s. Around North Dakota State University, older houses have been split into rentals. Head south past Interstate 94 and the streets turn into postwar ranches, then split-levels, then the large homes and townhouse rows of Osgood and the newest south Fargo additions. Each of those housing types breaks in its own way, and each produces its own kind of disputed claim.</p>
<h3>A storm record people still talk about</h3>
<p>The June 20, 1957 tornado, rated F5, cut through north Fargo and is still a reference point for local families. Most Fargo claims today come from more routine but costly events: summer hailstorms between roughly May and August, straight-line wind that strips shingles and siding, and long cold snaps that freeze supply lines in exterior walls. Winter brings ice dams on older homes with limited attic insulation and ventilation, and heavy snow load on low-slope commercial roofs.</p>
<p>The river is the other constant. The 2009 Red River flood pushed Fargo to a record crest of about 40.8 feet in late March 2009, and the city has spent years since then building levees and buying out flood-prone lots. Keep in mind that a standard homeowners policy excludes flood. Rising river water is generally a matter for a separate NFIP or private flood policy, and appraisal under a homeowners policy cannot turn an excluded loss into a covered one.</p>
<h3>Where Fargo claims usually go sideways</h3>
<p>Appraisal decides the amount of a covered loss, not whether the loss is covered. Within that lane, these are the gaps we see most often:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Roof scope.</strong> Hail-bruised shingles on one or two slopes get approved while matching slopes are left out, or the estimate skips drip edge, ice-and-water shield and other items that local code enforcement may expect on a full replacement.</li>
<li><strong>Siding and matching.</strong> Steel and vinyl siding on 1970s and 1980s homes is often discontinued. Patching one elevation with a near match leaves a visible difference, and whether the policy requires a reasonable match is a real question to raise.</li>
<li><strong>Interior water from freeze-ups.</strong> A burst line inside a plaster wall on a 1920s house is not priced like drywall in a 2015 build. Estimates that treat them the same come in low.</li>
<li><strong>Basements.</strong> Finished lower levels are common across Fargo. Covered water losses there involve flooring, trim, drywall, insulation and drying costs that are easy to undercount.</li>
</ul>
<h3>How we work a Fargo file</h3>
<p>North Dakota Insurance Appraiser starts with your policy, the carrier’s estimate and photos of the damage, then builds an independent line-by-line valuation using current pricing for the Fargo-Moorhead market. If the gap is worth pursuing and your policy has an appraisal clause, we can serve as your appraiser, negotiate with the carrier’s appraiser and, where needed, present the file to an umpire. We do not promise a result. Our job is to document what the repair actually costs and argue it clearly.</p>
<p>If you are unsure whether appraisal fits your situation, an estimate review is a sensible first step. Call <strong>+1 (844) 537-1427</strong> and North Dakota Insurance Appraiser will tell you plainly whether we think the numbers are worth contesting.</p>
<h2>Services available in Fargo</h2>
<p>Every North Dakota Insurance Appraiser service is available in Fargo and across Cass County, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/appraisal-representation/appraisal-clause-appointment/">Appraisal Clause Appointment</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-representation/home-condo-claim-appraisal/">Home &amp; Condo Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-representation/commercial-multifamily-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial, HOA &amp; Multifamily Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-representation/hail-roof-siding-appraisal/">Hail, Roof &amp; Siding Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-representation/wind-derecho-tornado-appraisal/">Wind, Derecho &amp; Tornado Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-representation/water-burst-pipe-appraisal/">Water &amp; Burst Pipe Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-representation/fire-smoke-grass-fire-appraisal/">Fire, Smoke &amp; Grass Fire Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-representation/freeze-ice-dam-snow-load-appraisal/">Freeze, Ice Dam &amp; Snow Load Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Questions from Fargo property owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My Fargo basement took on water during spring melt. Can appraisal help?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on the source. Surface or river flooding is excluded by standard homeowners policies and usually falls under a separate flood policy. Water from a burst pipe or a covered sewer backup endorsement may be covered, and if the carrier accepts that part but underpays it, appraisal can address the amount.</p></div></details><details><summary>My south Fargo roof was approved for repair, not replacement. Is that an appraisal issue?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Often, yes. When the dispute is about how much of a covered roof needs work, such as how many slopes or squares, that is a question of the amount of loss. We review the hail evidence, slope by slope, before recommending anything.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you handle rental houses near NDSU?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. Landlord and dwelling policies frequently contain appraisal clauses, and older rentals near campus often have plaster, original trim and layered roofing that carriers underestimate.</p></div></details></div>
